CAT = "s65Portfolio"; SEG = "PRT"
Q = [
("The risk-free rate is 3%, the market return is 8%, and a stock's beta is 1.4. Under CAPM, its expected return is:", '10%', ['11.2%', '14.2%', '8%'], 'CAPM: 3% + 1.4 x (8% - 3%) = 3% + 7% = 10%. Multiplying the whole market return by beta (11.2%) skips the risk-free step.'),
('Under Modern Portfolio Theory, adding an asset with low correlation to the rest of a portfolio usually:', 'Lowers overall risk', ['Raises both risk and return', 'Removes all market risk', 'Has no effect on risk'], 'Combining assets that do not move together cuts total volatility. Market risk still remains, because diversification only removes unsystematic risk.'),
('Portfolios that give the highest expected return for each level of risk lie on the:', 'Efficient frontier', ['Security market line', 'Yield curve', 'Capital gains line'], 'Modern Portfolio Theory plots optimal portfolios along the efficient frontier. Any portfolio below it could earn more for the same risk.'),
('A believer in the semi-strong form of the efficient market hypothesis thinks that:', 'Public data is already priced in', ['Insider information is priced in', 'Chart patterns predict prices', 'Fundamental analysis beats the market'], 'Semi-strong efficiency says prices already reflect all public information, so neither technical nor fundamental analysis gives an edge. Only the strong form includes inside information.'),
("An adviser sets a client's long-term mix at 60% stocks and 40% bonds and rebalances to it each year. This is:", 'Strategic allocation', ['Tactical asset allocation', 'Sector rotation', 'Market timing'], "Strategic allocation sets long-term targets from the client's goals and returns to them by rebalancing. Tactical allocation makes short-term shifts away from the targets."),
('An adviser temporarily moves from 60% to 70% stocks because she expects equities to outperform this year. This is:', 'Tactical asset allocation', ['Strategic asset allocation', 'Passive management', 'Dollar-cost averaging'], 'A deliberate short-term tilt away from the long-term mix to exploit expected conditions is tactical allocation.'),
('A client wants low costs and believes managers rarely beat the market after fees. The best fit is:', 'A passive index fund', ['An active stock picker', 'A sector rotation fund', 'A market-timing service'], 'Passive management holds an index to match its return at low cost. It suits investors who accept market returns rather than paying to try to beat them.'),
('A manager buys companies with low price-to-earnings and price-to-book ratios that seem out of favor. This style is:', 'Value investing', ['Growth investing', 'Momentum investing', 'Income investing'], 'Value managers look for stocks priced cheaply relative to earnings or assets. Growth managers pay higher multiples for faster earnings growth.'),
('A retiree wants regular cash from her portfolio with modest growth. The style that fits best is:', 'Income', ['Aggressive growth', 'Capital appreciation', 'Momentum'], 'An income style favors bonds, dividend-paying stocks and similar holdings that produce steady cash flow.'),
('A manager shifts money into consumer staples and utilities as the economy heads into a downturn. This is:', 'Sector rotation', ['Dollar-cost averaging', 'Indexing', 'Buy and hold'], 'Sector rotation moves among industries as the business cycle changes. Defensive sectors such as staples and utilities tend to hold up better in slowdowns.'),
('A client invests $500 a month in a fund at prices of $20, $25 and $10. Her average cost per share is:', 'About $15.79', ['About $18.33', '$20.00', '$10.00'], 'She buys 25 + 20 + 50 = 95 shares for $1,500, so her average cost is $1,500 / 95 = about $15.79. The average price paid, $18.33, is higher because more shares were bought when the price was low.'),
('A client holds 1,000 shares with large gains and fears a short-term drop but does not want to sell. A put option would:', 'Set a floor on the value', ['Add income with no added risk', "Cap the shares' upside", 'Lower her cost basis'], 'Buying protective puts locks in a minimum sale price until expiration, at the cost of the premium. Writing covered calls would add income but cap the upside.'),
('An investor writes covered calls on stock she owns. Her main trade-off is that she:', 'Caps gains above the strike', ['Takes on unlimited upside risk', 'Loses her voting rights', 'Pays a premium each month'], 'The call premium adds income, but if the stock rises past the strike the shares are called away, so the upside is capped. The downside below the premium remains.'),
('A client uses margin to buy twice as many shares as her cash allows. If the stock falls 15%, her equity falls about:', '30%', ['15%', '7.5%', '45%'], 'With 2:1 leverage, losses are magnified: a 15% drop in the position is about a 30% drop in her equity, before interest.'),
('High frequency trading firms mainly profit from:', 'Speed on many tiny trades', ['Holding stocks for years', 'Analyzing annual reports in depth', 'Buying IPO allocations'], 'HFT firms use fast computers and co-located servers to capture tiny price differences across a very large number of trades.'),
('A client places an order to sell her stock if it falls to $42, to cap her loss. She has placed a:', 'Sell stop order', ['Sell limit order', 'Buy stop order', 'Market-on-close order'], 'A sell stop sits below the market and becomes a market order once the price trades at or through the stop. A sell limit sits above the market.'),
('A quote shows a bid of $31.40 and an ask of $31.46. A customer selling at the market will most likely receive:', '$31.40', ['$31.46', '$31.43', '$31.52'], 'Sellers receive the bid, the price dealers are willing to pay; buyers pay the ask. The six-cent gap is the spread.'),
('A client buys 1,000 shares at the ask of $25.12 when the bid is $25.05. If she sold right away at the bid, her cost from the spread alone would be:', '$70', ['$7', '$140', '$25'], 'The spread is $25.12 - $25.05 = $0.07 a share, so 1,000 shares lose $70 on a round trip before any commission or markup. Doubling it ($140) counts the spread twice.'),
('A retail broker routes customer orders to a wholesaler that pays it for the order flow. The broker must still:', 'Seek best execution', ['Charge no commissions', 'Route only to exchanges', 'Hide the payment'], "Payment for order flow must be disclosed, and the broker's duty of best execution for each order still applies."),
('An introducing broker-dealer differs from a clearing firm because the introducing firm:', 'Holds no customer assets', ['Executes all trades itself', 'Acts as the exchange', 'Sets the market price'], 'Introducing firms deal with customers and pass orders and accounts to a clearing firm, which settles trades and holds the cash and securities.'),
("A manager's returns should be judged without the effect of client deposits and withdrawals. The right measure is:", 'Time-weighted return', ['Dollar-weighted return', 'Holding period return', 'Current yield'], 'Time-weighted return strips out the timing of cash flows the manager does not control, which is why it is the standard for comparing managers.'),
('An investor buys a stock for $50, collects $2 in dividends, and sells it a year later for $54. Her holding period return is:', '12%', ['8%', '4%', '10%'], 'Total return = (price change + income) / cost = ($4 + $2) / $50 = 12%. Leaving out the dividend gives 8%.'),
('An investment grew 21% over two years. Its annualized return is about:', '10%', ['10.5%', '21%', '11%'], 'Annualized return = (1.21)^(1/2) - 1 = 1.10 - 1 = 10%. Simply halving 21% gives 10.5%, which ignores compounding.'),
('A stock priced at $80 pays a $2.80 annual dividend. Its current yield is:', '3.5%', ['2.8%', '28.6%', '3.0%'], 'Current yield = annual income / price = $2.80 / $80 = 3.5%.'),
]
